\title{Depth Decay of the Magnitude Channel on the Berggren Tree of Primitive Pythagorean Triples}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{24 August 2026}

\begin{abstract}
Primitive Pythagorean triples are organized by Berggren's ternary tree, and in the standard $(m,n)$ parametrization the descent from a triple towards the root $(3,4,5)$ is governed entirely by the position of the ratio $r=m/n$ relative to the two cut points $2$ and $3$. The resulting address word over $\{A,B,C\}$ is a Gauss-map style digit expansion of $r$. We determine exactly how much of that address is legible to a fixed-precision magnitude sensor $P_W(m,n)=\lfloor 2^{W}m/n\rfloor$, whose budget $W$ grows neither with the size nor with the depth of the state.

Positively: the first descent letter is an explicit function of the one-bit reading $\lfloor 2m/n\rfloor$; the leading run of translation letters has length exactly $L=\lfloor (m-n)/(2n)\rfloor$, and any two admissible states with equal one-bit readings agree on all letters $0,\dots,L$, i.e.\ on the whole run together with the inversion letter terminating it; along the pure-translation spine this makes arbitrarily many letters readable; and depth is metered, $L\le P_1(m,n)/4$.

Negatively: for every budget $W$ and every depth $k$ there are admissible states with identical readings, identical addresses $C^{k}B$ through position $k$, and different letters at position $k+1$; such pairs exist at arbitrarily large scale; the same failure holds for every rational rescaling $\lfloor (a/b)(m/n)\rfloor$, by a construction using an \emph{attained} boundary and the right-continuity of the floor function; and a pigeonhole capacity bound forces collisions as soon as $2\cdot2^{W}<2^{k}$. The threshold is sharp: depth one is always readable, depth two is null for every budget.

The mechanism is that the translation branch acts on ratios as an isometry (transparent to truncation) while the two inversion branches are expansive (opaque), and that the only dyadic branch boundary is the top-level cut point $2$. We read these results as a quantitative sealing statement for cryptographic shortcut attempts on the Pythagorean tree.
\end{abstract}

